Generic `impl` Blocks — `impl[T] Trait[Box[T]]`
================================================

Per reference §"Traits and `impl` blocks" — generic impl blocks:

```sysl
trait Reader[I]
    type Token
    head(i: I) -> Self::Token

impl[T] Reader[Box[T]]
    type Token = T
    head(b: Box[T]) -> T = b.value
```

- An `impl` may itself be generic. The `impl[T1, T2, ...]` clause
  introduces tvars that appear in the trait's target type pattern.
- Each concrete substitution of the tvars produces a distinct
  monomorphized method.
- Trait calls infer the concrete substitution from argument types
  and dispatch to the matching generic impl.
- Bounds on impl tvars (`impl[T: Ord] Container[Box[T]]`) are
  checked at dispatch time when T is pinned.

This file pins:

- `impl[T] Trait[Box[T]]` over a generic struct
- Dispatching the same generic impl with multiple concrete T
- Multi-tvar generic impl (`impl[A, B] Trait[Pair[A, B]]`)
- Generic impl coexisting with a concrete impl on a different
  target shape

File-local prefix `Tgi` (Trait-Generic-Impl).

    module sysl.tests.lang_features


A generic wrapper struct
-------------------------

    struct TgiBox[T]
        v: T


### Generic impl over `TgiBox[T]`

    trait TgiUnbox[T]
        tgi_unbox(b: T) -> int

    impl[T] TgiUnbox[TgiBox[T]]
        tgi_unbox(b: TgiBox[T]) -> int = 1

### Dispatch with concrete instantiations

    #test
    test_tgi_concrete_dispatch() -> unit
        val b1 = TgiBox[int](42)
        val r = TgiUnbox.tgi_unbox(b1)
        assert(r == 1, s"got $r")


A trait that returns the element via the box
---------------------------------------------

    trait TgiPeek[T, R]
        tgi_peek(b: T) -> R

    impl[T] TgiPeek[TgiBox[T], T]
        tgi_peek(b: TgiBox[T]) -> T = b.v

### Generic impl returns the right element across concrete Ts

    #test
    test_tgi_peek_int() -> unit
        val b = TgiBox[int](42)
        val r = TgiPeek.tgi_peek(b)
        assert(r == 42, s"got $r")

    #test
    test_tgi_peek_bool() -> unit
        val b = TgiBox[bool](true)
        val r = TgiPeek.tgi_peek(b)
        assert(r, "got true")


Multi-tvar generic impl: `impl[A, B] Trait[Pair[A, B]]`
--------------------------------------------------------

    struct TgiPair[A, B]
        first: A
        second: B

    trait TgiFirst[T, R]
        tgi_first(p: T) -> R

    impl[A, B] TgiFirst[TgiPair[A, B], A]
        tgi_first(p: TgiPair[A, B]) -> A = p.first

    #test
    test_tgi_multi_tvar() -> unit
        val p = TgiPair[int, bool](7, true)
        val r = TgiFirst.tgi_first(p)
        assert(r == 7, s"got $r")


Generic impl coexisting with a concrete impl
---------------------------------------------

`TgiUnbox[TgiBox[T]]` (the generic impl above) handles any box;
adding `TgiUnbox[int]` (a concrete impl on a non-box type) tests
that the dispatcher picks the right one by operand-type shape.

    impl TgiUnbox[int]
        tgi_unbox(x: int) -> int = 999

    #test
    test_tgi_generic_with_concrete() -> unit
        val b = TgiBox[int](10)
        assert(TgiUnbox.tgi_unbox(b) == 1, "generic-impl path: 1")
        assert(TgiUnbox.tgi_unbox(42) == 999, "concrete-impl path: 999")


Generic impl method called from a regular function
---------------------------------------------------

The function takes a concrete `TgiBox[int]` and routes through the
generic impl.

    tgi_use_box(b: TgiBox[int]) -> int
        TgiPeek.tgi_peek(b) + 1

    #test
    test_tgi_called_from_function() -> unit
        val b = TgiBox[int](100)
        assert(tgi_use_box(b) == 101, "100+1=101")


Generic impl returning a constructed `TgiBox[T]`
-------------------------------------------------

    trait TgiBump[T]
        tgi_bump(b: T) -> T

    impl[T] TgiBump[TgiBox[T]]
        tgi_bump(b: TgiBox[T]) -> TgiBox[T] = TgiBox[T](b.v)

    #test
    test_tgi_bump_int() -> unit
        val b = TgiBox[int](7)
        val r = TgiBump.tgi_bump(b)
        assert(r.v == 7, s"got ${r.v}")

    #test
    test_tgi_bump_bool() -> unit
        val b = TgiBox[bool](false)
        val r = TgiBump.tgi_bump(b)
        assert(!r.v, "got false")
